+  // raptormark: `bti c` alone is NOT a function boundary, so the bound above does
+  // not do what its comment claims. `bti c` marks INDIRECT-CALL targets; a
+  // function nothing indirect-calls begins with `paciasp` and carries no landing
+  // pad at all. Measured on the fused images: 2,213 of bin_echo's 3,890 entries
+  // and 37,065 of postgres's 60,290 start with `paciasp`, so the scan runs
+  // straight through them.
+  //
+  // What it then seeds is an INTERIOR address of a later function, and decoding
+  // from an interior address walks that function's whole body into the caller.
+  // `__vfscanf` (60 bytes, 15 instructions) scanned 36,444 bytes to the next
+  // `bti c`, seeded 33 pads all interior to `_ecv_fde_657240`, and was emitted
+  // with 8,929 basic blocks -- 8,913 of them the callee's. The callee's ENTRY is
+  // never seeded, so the `bl` to it is still a call and the copy carries no entry
+  // block, which is the fingerprint that identified this.
+  //
+  // The symbol table already answers "where does the next function start", it
+  // needs no `st_size` (so unlike a `GetFuncVMA_E` bound it cannot truncate a
+  // function whose declared size understates its body -- that would cut 20 real
+  // functions on bash and 11 on postgres), and an in-function gap such as
+  // `base_yyparse`'s ~40 KB action table stays inside the entry-to-next-entry
+  // range by construction, so the scan keeps doing its job.
+  //
+  // `disasm_funcs` ONLY, deliberately. `rest_disasm_funcs` is filled during
+  // lifting, so bounding by it would make the answer depend on lift order and the
+  // emitted bitcode would stop being deterministic -- which the object cache and
+  // `patches/0029` both rely on. Excluding it can only leave the bound wider than
+  // necessary, never narrower.
+  const auto next_fn = disasm_funcs.lower_bound(static_cast<uintptr_t>(lo));
+  if (next_fn != disasm_funcs.end()) {
+    limit = std::min(limit, static_cast<uint64_t>(next_fn->first));
+  }
 
   for (auto it = std::lower_bound(bti_j_vmas.begin(), bti_j_vmas.end(), lo);
        it != bti_j_vmas.end() && *it < limit; ++it) {
